{
  "requirement": "`habeas status` and the mdBook requirements index MUST generate their Definition/Evidence Markdown tables through the same renderer. That renderer MUST calculate column widths, align the columns, and display the same evidence-state labels in both outputs. Callers MAY select different definitions and supply different kind-labeled name cells so mdBook can link to detail pages.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement is met: both outputs go through one renderer, and tests cover alignment and state labels in both. Coverage of the `habeas status` side is indirect, and width is measured in bytes (details below).\n\n**Implementation**\n- `src/markdown.rs` `render_evidence_table(rows: &[(String, &str)])` is the single renderer. It computes each column's width as the widest cell, with \"Definition\" and \"Evidence\" as minimums. It left-pads the header, separator and data rows to those widths.\n- `habeas status` (`src/commands.rs`, `status_markdown_with_options`, around lines 95–96) builds rows of `` `name` (kind) `` plus `evidence.state().label()`. It passes them to `render_evidence_table`.\n- The mdBook index (`src/index.rs`, `IndexPlan::populate`, around lines 351–352) builds rows of `` [`name`](href) (kind) `` plus `evidence.state().label()`. It passes them to the same `render_evidence_table`.\n- Both callers take the label from `EvidenceState::label()`, so the labels are identical in both outputs.\n- Callers choose their own definitions and their own name cells. `status` filters by glob and state. The index links each name to its detail page. This matches the \"MAY select different definitions and supply different kind-labeled name cells\" clause.\n\n**Tests**\n- `src/index.rs` `index_shows_evidence_states_and_links_generated_chapters` is tagged `{test}requirements.cli.shared_status_rendering`. It builds an index with five different states. It checks each row's linked name cell and its label (Valid, Determination false, Invalid, Missing). It also checks that every table line has its `|` delimiters at identical positions.\n- `tests/commands.rs` `status_tables_align_each_column_to_its_widest_cell` is tagged `requirements.cli.status_table_alignment`. It covers alignment for the `habeas status` side. Other status tests in `tests/commands.rs` check rows and labels such as `FIXME` and `(requirement)`.\n- Because both callers use the same function, these tests together cover the shared-renderer behaviour. There is no dedicated unit test of `render_evidence_table`.\n\n**Minor observations (not gaps)**\n- Width is measured with `.len()` (bytes), not characters. This could misalign for non-ASCII names, but definition names are ASCII in practice.\n- The `status` test is tagged only with `status_table_alignment`, not this requirement. The shared-renderer property is still demonstrated through the index test and the single code path.",
